From a22f7a8988dcf4c940b24c975b7b59c67a078cc2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logdog2325 <226450061+logdog2325@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:47:59 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 146/146] XD Netplay v1.7.0: change the battle music and
 location while XD is running

The room's Music & Location used to switch off once a battle was starting,
because the Battle Style block is synced once, at Start. It now works while
the game runs: the host picks, and both machines install the change at the
same emulated moment, taking effect from the next battle.

How it stays in step. Netplay's pad streams are already the session's
lockstep clock: every machine pops each pad's entries in the same order, so
pop number K of a pad is the same emulated poll everywhere (this depends on
the two fixes before it, which stop a machine losing entries at the start of
a game). A live change is numbered with the index of the next entry the host
will push for the first pad it owns, and sent right then. That entry leaves
afterwards on the same ordered channel, so every other machine registers the
change before the entry can arrive; the host registers it before pushing the
entry. A change is due from pop K on, and each machine installs it at the
first due pop where XD is between battles (the battle object's active-battler
pointer, 0x804AF528, reads 0 from the end of one battle to the first action of
the next). That memory is identical on every machine at the same
pop, so every machine picks the same pop, and a change made mid-battle waits
for the battle to end: XD reads a battle's terrain from the link record when a
move like Nature Power needs it, so moving the location mid-battle would
change the rules of the fight in progress. Several changes due at once
collapse to the newest; each is a full set. One window stays open: the few
seconds of a battle's intro, after setup has written the record and before the
first action. A location change landing there makes that battle's terrain
follow the new location while the old stage shows; both machines agree.

 - MessageID::LiveStyle (0xA8), host -> server -> everyone else. The server
   accepts it from the host only and only for the running game, the same gate
   as PadData, and relays it synchronously in arrival order.
 - NetPlayClient::RequestLiveStyle (any thread) leaves the request for the CPU
   thread; PollLocalPad numbers and sends it; GetNetPads applies it before the
   numbered pop. Push and pop counters restart with the pad queues in
   OnStartGame. m_live_mutex is a leaf lock, never held while taking another,
   and nothing new runs NetPlay:: free functions under crit_netplay_client.
   Only 0x04 and 0x02 write lines into main memory are accepted, at most 64.
   Live changes exist only in Pokemon XD rooms (GXXE01, the room's synced
   game) and are refused in RetroAchievements hardcore mode, a synced setting.
 - ActionReplay gets a live code slot, run after the active codes every frame
   so it wins on shared addresses, whether or not cheats are enabled (it only
   comes from netplay; a default-style session has cheats off), cleared by
   PatchEngine::Shutdown. It never looks at this machine's own achievements
   state, which is local and could let one machine run it and not the other.
 - BattleCustomizer::MakeLiveStyle builds the live set from the Start block's
   own music and location lines. "Game default" music writes back the vanilla
   staBGM_tunaide value (0x4F3, read from a clean main.dol) so it overrides a
   Start pin. Music also goes into the four link records' own BGM field
   (+0x10), which the battle reads once at its start: a rematch re-runs battle
   setup only when XD's stage row is on Random, so otherwise a rematch would
   keep the old music. A location pinned only live is lifted cleanly: the game writes
   the link records' battlefield itself at each battle start, and the stage
   row's blanked sprite is restored to its main.dol value (0x011600A7). A
   location the Start block pinned cannot be lifted, since that block keeps
   writing it, so "Game default" then keeps the current location and says it
   applies from the next Start. MakeLiveStyle changes nothing itself; the
   callers commit its bookkeeping (CommitLiveStyle) only once the client has
   accepted the set, and only the netplay host's Start paths reset it
   (BeginLiveStyleForStart, unconditionally: an Android host can press Start
   while its previous game is still closing).
 - The launcher's solo Boot (desktop OnBootSolo, Android nativePrepareForBoot)
   now does nothing while a game runs. The desktop launcher stays open beside a
   room it hosted, and PrepareForStart would rewrite GXXE01.ini and could set
   the cheats flag on one machine only in the middle of a netplay game.
 - The picks are saved for the next Start, but the INI is not regenerated
   while a game runs: nothing may change what this machine would boot while a
   game started from it is still running.

UI. Desktop: the button stays enabled while a game runs, and a start in
flight is still refused. Android: the room's button likewise, plus a Music &
Location entry in the in-game menu for the host, because leaving the game
for the room pauses the core and freezes the other player; that entry shows
only for the host of a Pokemon XD room. The room's dialog reads the stored
picks each time it opens, since the in-game menu can change them. No chat
line is posted when a change arrives: that would come from the netplay thread
mid-battle, and reach the on-screen chat during the GBA link.

Logs: "livestyle send/recv at=K ops=N sum=..." and "livestyle apply at=K
pop=P ..." on each machine, so the two logs show the same change landing at
the same pop P; "livestyle LATE" if a change ever arrived after its pop.

Models, teams and the timer stay between-games only for now.
